// DOUBLE TAKE FRAMES — DETACHABLE-ROOF MAIN FRAME
// Based on current pending main-frame-v2.0.4-pending
// Versioning: Semantic Versioning 2.0.0 (https://semver.org/)
// Status: CANONICAL — dry-cassette print candidate
// Experimental scope:
// - removes the integral roof and its acrylic/paper loading slots;
// - preserves the existing internal sheet guides and rear fangs unchanged;
// - adds four short corner slide retainers inset from the front/rear edges;
// - includes the complete roof as a separate body in this same source.
// Changes in 2.0.4-rc.1:
// - Strengthens the clock-guide receiver detents so retention relies less on
//   compressing and bowing the guide between its end recesses.
// - Saves the pending main-frame output as the wet cassette configuration.
// - Replaces the broad upper reservoir and its outlet transition with one
//   narrow, open-bottom water chimney continuous with the lower receiver.
// - Uses the cassette's existing left wall as the lower receiver's outside
//   wall and keeps the small PVA chamber inset from the rear envelope.
// - Removes the obsolete upper wet-side string-guide passage; string handling
//   remains entirely at the dedicated wetting and hitching structure below.
// - Extends the narrow receiver column to the cassette's left wall, removing
//   its redundant separate side wall without disturbing outlet alignment.
// - Extends only the low receiver boot to the cassette's -Y front wall below
//   the image channel; the common chimney/receiver width eliminates the old
//   exposed ledge and its print-sensitive gussets.
// - Opens the small string-wetting chamber for a sponge insert and enlarges
//   its string passage by one 0.6 mm nozzle width for easier threading.
// - Adds narrow rear-entry slots into both string holes so the string can be
//   loaded sideways, perpendicular to its primarily vertical tension.
// - Trims the legacy lateral wings from the two top backing-board retainers,
//   leaving only the bridge width required by the functional snap return.
// - Removes the remaining rear overhang beyond the functional top-retainer
//   return and adds a wet/non-dry floor gusset at the unsupported right span.
// - Replaces the guided-synapse wetting path with one open basin and two small
//   staggered channel cuts. The exposed PVA crosses the runoff without added
//   eyelet geometry shielding it from the water.
// - Feeds the basin directly from the shared water chimney through a small
//   printable internal passage. Hitch geometry is deliberately deferred.
// - Restores the chimney's sloped paper-deflection roof without extending it
//   over the separately accessible wetting unit.
// - Adds opposed side-entry hitch cuts to the shortened ramp-aligned block:
//   a smooth +Y wrap pocket and a narrowing -Y cleat for the thin PVA string.
// - Adds a shallow 45-degree outside lead-in to the commercial silicone-plug
//   drain while retaining 1.3 mm of straight 5.2 mm locking bore.
// - Restores the full untrimmed 9 mm capture geometry on both -X wall-rig
//   receivers; the former 4 mm lower and 2 mm upper clearance cuts are gone.
// - Vents the highest portion of the sloped water-chimney roof so displaced
//   air can escape while the silicone-plugged cassette is syringe-filled.
// - Adds a low, wall-rooted wet-string cleat in the expendable bottom paper
//   zone so the line can fall directly from the channel eyelet to its hitch.
// - Enlarges the enclosed wetting chamber and gives it four-nozzle-line walls.
//   One guide enters through the top and the other exits high on the +Y side;
//   their short exposed split sits near the chamber floor for PVA wetting.
// - Uses one compact 5 mm wall block whose roof slopes downward at 45 degrees
//   in +Y, stops at a blunt 1 mm nose, and encloses a straight 45-degree bore
//   from the +X side to the -Z bottom.
// - Removes the lower receiver's offset boot/riser transition and continues
//   the upper chimney's constant cross-section straight down to the floor.
// - Flares the chimney cavity into a continuous 1.2 mm wet-side base gusset
//   so the wall-to-floor junction is thicker and less prone to seepage.
// - Replaces the direct-water wetting box with a simple side-load PVA contact
//   eyelet and adds a second roof passage for an adjustable cotton capillary
//   wick. The original roof vent remains independent of the swollen wick.
// Changes in 2.0.3:
// - Carried forward unchanged for the corrected prepared-project packaging.
// Changes in 2.0.2:
// - Carried forward unchanged for the native 3MF packaging hotfix.
// Changes in 2.0.1:
// - Carried forward unchanged for the prepared-project packaging correction.
// Changes in 2.0.0:
// - Promotes the latest water-cassette geometry as the shared main-frame source.
// - Preserves roller insertion clearance by shortening the +Y backing-board
//   pocket return instead of extending the existing minimum-clearance -Y fang.
// - Clips the clock-guide retention ridges to their parent wall and gusset so
//   they cannot continue through empty space beyond the supporting material.
// - Extends the 10 mm-wide, 5 mm-proud rounded C-channel continuously into the
//   catch basin. A simple 45-degree tongue interrupts the runoff partway down;
//   its open keyhole seat accepts the PVA laterally without threading.
// - Keeps the RC3 -Y placement and the original plain structural center strip.
//   The level catch basin uses four 0.6 mm wall lines and a 3.6 mm floor; no
//   second drain or drain-footprint accommodation is included.
// - Defaults to the combined dry/wet cassette configuration. Either subsystem
//   can still be disabled independently for isolated review.
// Full release notes: https://doubletakeframes.com/build/#design-release
